\section{Midpoint and width}{

The sigmoid is placed by \code{midpoint}, the intensity at which \eqn{F = 0.5},
and scaled by \code{width}, the distance on the intensity axis between
\eqn{F = 0.05} and \eqn{F = 0.95} (Schütt et al., 2016). Both mean the same
thing for every sigmoid, so estimates are comparable across sigmoids.

\code{midpoint} is not the threshold at a target performance level: because of
the guess and lapse rates, \eqn{F = 0.5} is \eqn{\psi = 0.75} in 2AFC
without lapses, but \eqn{\psi = 0.5} in a yes/no task. Use
\code{\link[=psychometric_threshold]{psychometric_threshold()}} for the intensity at any performance level, with
its posterior, and for the slope there.
}

\section{Sigmoids}{

\itemize{
\item \code{"normal"}: cumulative normal (probit), on the intensity axis
\item \code{"logistic"}: logistic, on the intensity axis
\item \code{"gumbel_min"}: \eqn{1 - \exp(-\exp(z))}{1 - exp(-exp(z))}, skewed
towards low intensities (psignifit's \code{"gumbel"})
\item \code{"gumbel_max"}: \eqn{\exp(-\exp(-z))}{exp(-exp(-z))}, skewed towards
high intensities (psignifit's \code{"rgumbel"})
\item \code{"weibull"}: \code{"gumbel_min"} on log intensity, the Weibull function
\item \code{"lognormal"}, \code{"loglogistic"}: \code{"normal"} and \code{"logistic"} on log
intensity
}
The last three read the intensity on the log scale, so it must be positive.
Their \code{midpoint} is still reported in the units of the intensity, through a
log link, while their \code{width} is in log units.
}

\section{Guess and lapse rates}{

Each rate is either estimated, with a logit link and its own formula, or
fixed by the design. Fix them in the call, on the probability scale, not in
the formula: \code{guess = 0.5} for 2AFC, \code{guess = 1 / m} for m-AFC,
\code{guess = "chance"} to read a per-row guess rate from a column (for designs
that mix 2AFC and 4AFC trials), \code{lapse = 0} for no lapses. A fixed value of
zero is allowed. Leave \code{guess} estimated for yes/no tasks, where it is the
false-alarm rate far below the midpoint.

The lapse rate of Wichmann and Hill (2001), and of psignifit and Palamedes,
is the gap between the upper asymptote and 1, which is
\code{lapse * (1 - guess)} here: in 2AFC, half the \code{lapse} this model reports.
\code{\link[=psychometric_threshold]{psychometric_threshold()}} returns both.
}

\section{Default priors}{

Stimulus units are arbitrary, so the priors on \code{midpoint} and \code{width} are
scaled to the range of the intensities in the data (on the log scale for the
log-scale sigmoids), as psignifit does. With \eqn{c} the centre and \eqn{R}
the range, the \code{midpoint} intercept gets \code{normal(c, R / 2)}, its effects
\code{normal(0, R / 4)} and its random-effect SDs \code{exponential(4 / R)}; the log of
\code{width} gets \code{normal(log(R / 2), 1)}. The priors listed above are these for a
range of 0 to 1; \code{\link[=default_prior]{default_prior()}} shows the ones for your data.
}

